  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1006 PM MDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nAs low pressure finally lifts out onto the Plains early this\nmorning, the persistent stream of remnant tropical storm\nmoisture goes with it. Flash flood Watches were cancelled as \nrivers continue to recede. Rain soaked portions of the CWA will \nfinally see some sunshine on Thursday as high pressure builds \ninto the region. Parts of the Four Corners could see some \nstratus ceilings and fog develop early Thursday morning, given \nthe pool of surface moisture laying around from 2 days of rain. \nShould fog build into the area, those locales will have to wait \non sunshine until around mid day. Areas under clear skies will \nsee temperatures surge into the normal range and above heading \ninto the weekend, as high pressure arcs over the West. Highs \npeak out Sunday around 5-10 degrees over normal. Light winds and\nquiet weather conditions will make for a rather nice weekend \nfor leaf peepers.\n\nA couple of features materialize in the models late in the\nweekend that hint at some additional showers. Nothing in the\nform of a major pattern shift emerges though. A pinched off\nlow/open wave works through the Great Basin late Sunday.\nMeanwhile, whatever is left of Hurricane Rachel lurks along the\nBaja coast. Some model guidance tries to lift a push of moisture\ninto the region Sunday afternoon, setting off some showers on\nthe San Juans. This looks like a very tentative solution, but\nthe models have been selling it over several runs now. This\ntrend continues on Monday and Tuesday with the potential for\nmore widespread showers on the mountains. There's a lot of room\nfor this forecast to come apart by then, so we'll hold off on\nbold predictions and enjoy a few days of mild fall weather.",
